{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Build a behavioral CONFORMANCE ORACLE that mechanically asserts write-op parity across the lib, CLI, and MCP adapters, so surface drift is caught without an LLM, without a change-detector, and without a hand-maintained NxM matrix. This is the durable form of the validated prototype (docs/experiments/write_parity_oracle_prototype.py).\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe write ops (transition/claim/create) are exposed through three adapters — the Python lib, the CLI, and the MCP server — but their input surfaces are hand-mirrored. A param present on one can silently be missing on another (exactly the MCP gaps captured in sibling scratchy-leprous-galago: reason/caused_by/ref/force). Nothing mechanically guards against this drift today; it is caught only by chance or by LLM review.\n\n## Approach\nPromote the prototype into a durable pytest CONFORMANCE ORACLE (Pattern B, proven in OSS: Python DB-API / SQLAlchemy dialect compliance, the CommonMark spec suite, WSGI/ASGI validators). ONE transport-agnostic contract table holds rows of (op, inputs, expected accept | reject-with-rule, expected effect). Each row is executed through every adapter against a fresh temp store and classified ACCEPTED / REJECTED(rule) / PARAM_NOT_EXPOSED; any divergence between adapters on the same row fails the suite. This asserts BEHAVIOR — including the runtime conditional-required rules (close_class required iff bug-close; reason required for REASON_REQUIRED_CLASSES) — which shape/signature generation cannot express. We deliberately do NOT generate the surfaces (Pattern A): behavior is already single-sourced through composer.create_core / transition_compute / claim_compute, and the adapters carry irreducible hand-written value (MCP LLM-facing docstrings, CLI ergonomics), so regenerating them would risk agent/user-visible contract breaks. Known current MCP gaps are encoded as strict-xfail rows citing scratchy-leprous-galago, so the suite is GREEN in CI today and a gap-close makes the strict xfail fail (xpass) — forcing cleanup — instead of the suite depending on scratchy landing first.\n\n## Scope\n- tests/interfaces/test_write_parity_oracle.py — new conformance suite.\n- tests/interfaces/write_parity_contract.py — new shared contract table (the single source of cases).\n- Reuse the _FakeMCP/_FakeCtx capture (tests/unit/test_mcp_writes.py) and the interfaces sandbox-repo bootstrap (tests/interfaces/conftest.py).\n- Cover the transition, claim, and create write ops across all three adapters; add the CLI adapter driver over the CLI verb path.\n- Treat source/return_alias/_creation_channel/repo_root as adapter-bound internals (NOT contract params) so they do not register as false divergences.\n- No CI-provider-specific trigger: runs as an ordinary pytest (portability).\n\n## Testing\n- Run `pytest tests/interfaces/test_write_parity_oracle.py`: green in CI, with the current MCP reason/caused_by/force gaps recorded as strict-xfail rows referencing scratchy-leprous-galago.\n- Include one reason-required bug-close row and one close_class-missing row to assert the runtime conditional rules behaviorally on every adapter (both REJECTED consistently across adapters).\n- Include a negative-control row over an adapter-bound internal (e.g. source) to confirm it is NOT flagged as a divergence.\n- Confirm that locally simulating MCP parity flips the strict-xfail rows to xpass (i.e. the oracle would then require their removal).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A single shared contract table drives all three adapter drivers; adding a new op is one new row (verified by inspecting the module: one table, three drivers).\n- [ ] `pytest tests/interfaces/test_write_parity_oracle.py` passes in CI, with the current MCP reason/caused_by/force gaps captured as strict-xfail rows citing scratchy-leprous-galago (visible in the test output).\n- [ ] Runtime rules (close_class-required, reason-required) are asserted behaviorally for every adapter (the two rows exist and reject consistently).\n- [ ] Adapter-bound internals (source/return_alias/_creation_channel/repo_root) produce no false divergence (negative-control row passes).\n- [ ] Simulating MCP parity locally turns the strict-xfail rows to xpass, proving the oracle detects the fix (recorded in the ticket).\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` are green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787081929580357001,"uuid":"1b800e3a-e8a2-4917-aa9e-1e01002b58a8"}